 "api_error_status": null,
 "result": "Tessel (a Flask-like framework) uses renamed methods. The path, in order:\n\n1. **`Tessel.__call__`** (`src/tessel/app.py:1618`) just delegates to `wsgi_entry`.\n2. **`Tessel.wsgi_entry`** (`src/tessel/app.py:1566`) is the real WSGI app. It builds the context and wraps the rest in try/finally.\n3. **`Tessel.request_context`** (`src/tessel/app.py:1501`) creates the `AppContext` from `environ`.\n4. **`AppContext.push`** (`src/tessel/ctx.py:416`) does the following:\n   - sets the context var;\n   - sends `appcontext_pushed`;\n   - opens the session via `_get_session`.\n5. **`AppContext.match_request`** (`src/tessel/ctx.py:405`) is called from `push` and runs the URL routing. It stores `url_rule` and `view_args`, or `routing_exception`.\n6. **`Tessel.run_full_request`** (`src/tessel/app.py:992`) sends the `request_started` signal.\n7. **`Tessel.preprocess_request`** (`src/tessel/app.py:1366`) runs the `url_value_preprocessors`, then the `before_request_funcs`. A non-None return short-circuits the view.\n8. **`Tessel.run_view`** (`src/tessel/app.py:966`) does the following:\n   - raises any routing exception;\n   - handles automatic OPTIONS;\n   - looks up `view_functions[rule.endpoint]`, wraps it in `ensure_sync`, and calls it with `view_args`. This is where the view runs.\n\nExceptions from steps 6–8 go to `route_user_fault`. After the view, `finalize_request` builds the response.",
